            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily identifies 'meat' as advanced spiritual teaching or deeper doctrine suited for mature believers, contrasted with the 'milk' of elementary teaching. It also recognizes an alternative meaning based on John 4 of doing God's will.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'meat' as the word obeyed to completeness, explicitly contrasting it with 'milk' which is merely the word received.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Advanced spiritual doctrine or mature teaching.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The active obedience and complete application of the word.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book reclassifies the primary apostolic milk/meat contrast (e.g., Hebrews 5:14) by rejecting the category of 'advanced teaching' and applying the 'doing God's will' definition (John 4:34) to all instances of spiritual meat.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus claims spiritual meat is a deeper level of teaching given to believers; the book claims it is the believer's own active obedience and practice of the word already received.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book elevates a common alternative reading (doing God's will) into a universal definition, displacing the consensus primary reading (advanced doctrine). Because doctrine (received teaching) and obedience (action) are different referents, the core materially diverges.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In 1 Corinthians 3:2, Paul states he 'fed' the Corinthians with milk and not meat. This strongly implies that meat is a type of instruction imparted by a teacher, which makes less sense if meat is defined as the listeners' own active obedience.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
